<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gudea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2100 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/22nd-century-bce">22nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gudea was a ruler of the Sumerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> around 2100 BCE, in the period between the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> and the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>. He is known for an unusual number of temple-building inscriptions and some two dozen diorite statues showing him in prayer, most of them now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea-cylinders">Gudea Cylinders</a> describe the rebuilding of the Eninnu temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gudea.jpg" alt="Gudea" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jastrow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gudea_of_Lagash_Girsu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Gudea ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> during the Second Dynasty of Lagash, after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> and before Lagash was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>. His reign is estimated at between 20 and 25 years, and its exact dates cannot be fixed. He bore the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ensi">ensi</a>, ruler or governor, and became ruler as the son-in-law of his predecessor Ur-Bau, having married his daughter Ninalla. His name means the one who is called, and may be a throne name that expresses his claim to have been chosen by the gods. After the end of Akkadian rule, southern Mesopotamia split apart; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutians">Gutians</a> held the north, and Ur-Namma later unified the region from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Gudea presents himself above all as a pious king. His ten attested year names and his inscriptions commemorate rebuilt temples, offerings to the gods and the consecration of a high priestess, and only one military campaign, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, is mentioned. He turned away from the warlike image of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, and historians often see his reign as the start of a Sumerian renaissance. His chief god was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a>, whose temple Eninnu at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> he rebuilt, and he made Ningishzida his personal god and claimed the goddesses Gatumdug and Ninsun as his divine mothers.</p><p>He is best known for his statues, most of them carved from diorite imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magan">Magan</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. Specialists put the corpus at between 24 and 27 statues, many of them headless. They were first found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Telloh</a>, ancient Girsu, in the French excavations of 1878 to 1909, and others turned up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> and Tell Hammam. His inscriptions and two clay cylinders are among the finest examples of Sumerian literature.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> was incorporated into the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> after his reign, apparently under his fifth successor Nam-mahani, probably peacefully. Gudea was deified and received offerings at festivals and a priest of his own, and his name was used in personal names. In the 2nd century BCE a local ruler named Adad-nadin-ahhe, who admired the past, built a palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Telloh</a> and moved several statues of Gudea there, where they remained until modern excavations. The objects Gudea left, unearthed in the last quarter of the 19th century, played a decisive part in the rediscovery of Sumerian civilisation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gudea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gudea</b> campaigned to the east against Anshan and Elam; his sixth year was named for the smiting of Anshan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gudea</b>, ruler of Lagash around 2100 BCE, left long Sumerian inscriptions on statues and clay cylinders, and the Third Dynasty of Ur, which ruled Mesopotamia from about 2112 to 2004 BCE, used Sumerian for a vast administration that has left tens of thousands of tablets, as well as…</blockquote><a href="/language/sumerian">Sumerian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Akkadian Empire, claimed that ships from Meluhha, Magan and Dilmun moored at the quay of his capital, Akkad, and <b>Gudea</b>, ruler of Lagash in the late 3rd millennium BCE, wrote of lapis lazuli and bright carnelian from Meluhha; other texts mention Meluhhan gold dust and fine woods.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Some kings, such as Erridupizir, left Akkadian inscriptions at Nippur imitating Akkadian royal style, but southern cities like Lagash under <b>Gudea</b> and Ur and Uruk carried on largely independently.</blockquote><a href="/state/gutian-rule">Gutian rule</a></li></ul></section>
